# ---------------------------------------------------------------- more advice
ADV = [
 ("How do I ask for help without feeling like a burden?", "Most people like to help when asked clearly. Be specific about what you need and how much time it takes, make it easy to say no, and thank them afterwards. You'd likely help a friend in the same position without a second thought."),
 ("What's the best way to start journaling?", "Keep it tiny: three lines before bed about what happened and how you felt. No rules, no perfection. Use prompts such as 'What went well today?' on days you're stuck."),
 ("How can I stop procrastinating on small tasks?", "Use the two-minute rule: if something takes under two minutes, do it now. For bigger small tasks, batch them into one 30-minute slot a day and treat finishing as the reward."),
 ("What makes a good first impression?", "Be on time, make eye contact, smile, listen more than you talk and remember names. Being genuinely interested in the other person usually beats trying to impress."),
 ("How do I deal with a boss who micromanages?", "Build trust with proactive updates so they don't need to ask, clarify expectations at the start of tasks and propose what you'll deliver and when. If it continues, have a calm conversation about the level of autonomy you need."),
 ("Should I keep a gratitude list?", "It can help mood for many people, especially if you write specifics rather than generic lines. Doing it a few times a week keeps it from feeling like a chore."),
 ("How do I handle feeling jealous of a friend's success?", "Notice the feeling without judging it; it usually points to something you want. Congratulate them sincerely, and then ask what small step you could take towards your own goal."),
 ("What's the best way to give up a bad habit?", "Make the habit harder (add friction), make a replacement easier, track streaks, and plan for the trigger moment. Be kind to yourself after a slip and restart right away."),
 ("How can I improve my focus at work?", "Work in blocks of 25 to 50 minutes with phone notifications off, start with the most demanding task while fresh, and keep a list of distractions to handle later."),
 ("Is it worth it to wake up earlier?", "Only if it lets you go to bed on time. The benefit comes from having a quiet, protected block, not the hour itself. Shift by 15 minutes at a time."),
 ("How do I forgive someone who hurt me?", "Forgiveness is a process, not a switch. It doesn't mean excusing what happened or reconciling. It can mean deciding not to carry the anger, and it may help to write down your feelings, even in a letter you don't send."),
 ("What should I do on my first day at a new school?", "Arrive early, bring the basics, smile, and ask for help finding your way. Introduce yourself to one or two people at lunch and be patient; friendships take a few weeks."),
 ("How do I make a long-distance relationship work?", "Agree on regular contact without making it a chore, share small daily details, plan visits ahead, and be honest about worries. Having an end date or a plan to close the distance helps."),
 ("How do I choose a gift for a coworker?", "Keep it modest and inclusive: a nice tea or chocolate, a plant, or something related to a hobby you've heard them mention. A short, warm note matters more than the price."),
 ("How do I stop worrying about what others think?", "Remember that people think about you far less than you imagine. Act on your values, ask whose opinion truly matters, and practise small things that feel slightly uncomfortable."),
 ("What's a good way to end a friendship that has become toxic?", "Be honest but gentle: you can slowly reduce contact or say clearly that the friendship isn't working for you. You don't owe a long explanation, but staying kind makes it easier on both sides."),
 ("How do I learn to enjoy exercise?", "Choose activities you find fun, not ones you think you should do. Make them social, track progress that isn't about weight, and focus on how you feel afterward."),
 ("How can I be a better manager?", "Listen more, give clear goals, give feedback early and specifically, protect your team's time and admit mistakes. Ask them what they need from you."),
 ("How do I prepare for retirement?", "Estimate your expected expenses, check what pensions you'll receive, save consistently, reduce high-interest debt and think about how you'll spend your time. A financial adviser can help with the details for your country."),
 ("What should I do if I feel burned out?", "Reduce load where you can, rest properly, tell someone at work and see a doctor if symptoms persist. Burnout rarely resolves with a weekend; it needs changes to workload and recovery."),
 ("How do I introduce myself in a networking event?", "Keep it to two sentences: who you are, what you do or are interested in, and a question to the other person. Listening and following up afterwards count more than a perfect pitch."),
 ("How can I get better at saying thank you?", "Be specific about what the person did and the effect it had: 'Thanks for staying late to help, it meant I could make the deadline.' A thoughtful sentence beats a generic one."),
 ("What are good habits for a clean inbox?", "Check at set times, unsubscribe from what you don't read, use two or three folders, and reply to anything under two minutes straight away. Treat the inbox as a place to process, not store."),
 ("How do I handle a friend who cancels plans a lot?", "Tell them how it feels, without blame, and ask what's going on. Make plans that are easy to keep or low-pressure, and decide what level of flakiness you can accept."),
 ("How do I plan a productive study session?", "Choose one clear goal for the session, set a timer for 25 to 50 minutes, start with active recall instead of rereading, take a short break and write what you learned in your own words."),
 ("What's a good way to teach a child to be kind?", "Model it yourself, name kind actions when you see them, include them in simple helping tasks and talk about how others feel. Praise effort and empathy, not just outcomes."),
 ("How can I stop being late all the time?", "Estimate with buffers: add 15 minutes to your usual travel time, prepare things the night before, set an alarm for leaving, not for arriving, and notice what usually makes you late."),
 ("How do I deal with noise when I'm trying to focus?", "Use noise-cancelling headphones, steady background sound or instrumental music, choose a quieter space or time, and tell people when you need uninterrupted time."),
 ("What's the best way to learn from failure?", "Review it without blame: what happened, what I controlled, what I'd do differently. Write down one change, then try again soon, since quick retries turn lessons into habits."),
 ("How do I support a friend who is going through a breakup?", "Listen without fixing, check in regularly, offer company or practical help like meals, and avoid bashing the ex. Let them lead how much they want to talk."),
]
for i, (u, a) in enumerate(ADV):
    for rep in range(3):
        sysm = [pick(R, SYS_GENERAL), None, pick(R, SYS_GENERAL[3:])][rep]
        uu = [u, R.choice(["Quick question: " + lf(u), lf(u)]), R.choice([u + " Be honest.", u + " Short answer is fine."])][rep]
        out.append(mk(CAT, "everyday_advice", [("user", uu), ("assistant", a)], f"cc3a_{i}", "medium", "authored" if rep == 0 else "composed", sysm))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- balanced opinions (framed as perspectives)
OP = [
 ("Is social media good or bad for people?", "It's both, depending on how it's used. It can help people stay connected, find communities and learn, but heavy use, comparison and endless scrolling are linked with worse mood for some, especially younger people. Using it intentionally, with time limits and a curated feed, tends to help."),
 ("Should kids have smartphones?", "There's no single right age. Considerations include the child's maturity, the safety features available, and family rules. Many families start with a basic phone, delay social media and keep devices out of bedrooms. It's worth deciding the rules together."),
 ("Is it better to rent or buy a home?", "It depends on local prices, how long you'll stay, your savings and your tolerance for maintenance. Buying builds equity but costs more upfront and reduces flexibility; renting is flexible but doesn't build ownership. Running the numbers for your area is the best way to decide."),
 ("Do you think college is worth it?", "For many people it pays off through higher earnings and opportunities, but it varies by field, cost and the person. Alternatives such as apprenticeships and trade training can be excellent in the right situation. It helps to compare the cost with realistic outcomes in your chosen field."),
 ("Is working from home better than the office?", "Each has strengths. Home offers focus and flexibility; the office offers collaboration and clearer boundaries for some. Hybrid arrangements try to combine both. The best answer depends on the work and the person."),
 ("Are electric cars really better for the environment?", "Over their lifetime they usually produce lower emissions than petrol cars, even counting battery production, and the gap widens as electricity gets cleaner. The details depend on where the electricity comes from and how long the car is used."),
 ("Should I follow my passion or choose a stable job?", "It doesn't have to be an either-or. Many people choose stability first and pursue passions on the side, or find a career where their interests and skills overlap. Consider your finances, risk tolerance and what you'd regret more."),
 ("Is it ever okay to lie?", "Ethical traditions differ. Most agree honesty is the default, but some allow exceptions such as protecting someone from harm. Small social white lies are common, though they can erode trust if they become habitual. Where do you draw the line?"),
 ("Do you think money buys happiness?", "Up to the point where basic needs and security are met, more money clearly improves wellbeing. Beyond that, the link is weaker, and how you spend it, such as on experiences and time, matters. People differ."),
 ("Is it better to specialise early or explore?", "Early specialisation can build deep skill quickly in some fields, such as music or sport, while exploring helps people find fit and creativity in many others. A reasonable middle path is to explore broadly, then commit once you know what you care about."),
 ("Should schools have homework?", "Evidence suggests moderate homework helps older students, while heavy loads for young children offer little benefit and can harm sleep and family time. Quality and purpose matter more than quantity."),
 ("Is a four-day work week a good idea?", "Trials report maintained or improved productivity and better wellbeing in many cases, but it depends on the type of work and how the schedule is designed. It isn't a fit for every business, and results vary."),
 ("Are video games bad for you?", "In moderation they can be fun, social and even good for problem-solving. Problems arise with excessive play that replaces sleep, exercise or relationships. It's about balance and the content of the game."),
 ("What's your view on minimalism?", "It can be freeing if it reduces clutter and spending and helps you focus on what matters. It's less helpful when it becomes a competition or a rule that creates stress. The point is intention, not owning the fewest things."),
 ("Should I trust online reviews?", "Use them as one input, not the verdict. Read a mix of positive and negative reviews, look for specific details, be wary of repeated phrases or sudden bursts of five-star ratings, and compare with independent sources."),
 ("Is multitasking a skill?", "Not really. People switch quickly between tasks, which costs time and increases errors. Some combinations work, like walking while listening to a podcast, but not tasks that both need attention."),
 ("Is it healthy to work out every day?", "It can be, if you vary intensity and include rest. Daily gentle movement such as walking is fine for most people, while hard training benefits from recovery days. Persistent pain or fatigue is a sign to ease off."),
 ("Do you think people can change?", "Yes, though change usually takes time, motivation and support. Habits and behaviours can shift, and sometimes values too, while certain traits tend to stay relatively stable. Small consistent steps matter most."),
]
for i, (u, a) in enumerate(OP):
    for rep in range(2):
        out.append(mk(CAT, "balanced_opinion", [("user", u if rep == 0 else R.choice(["What do you think? " + u, u + " Give me both sides."])), ("assistant", a)], f"cc3o_{i}", "medium", "authored" if rep == 0 else "composed", pick(R, SYS_GENERAL)))

save("conversation3", out)
